<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaSaltCrust.xml                      Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-13
  ============================================================================

  V1 ROW 4, override 1 of 3: "Three terrain or resource overrides that are
  visible on the map."

  ===========================================================================
  DESIGN INTENT FOLLOWED, NOT INVENTED
  ===========================================================================
  The salt flat is one of the six LOCKED terrain anchors and is the ONLY one
  with no def behind it anywhere in the stack. A retired seat wrote the intent and
  nobody built it:

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/desert_world_design.md:136
      "① Abundant: perfect flat build/landing pad + total visibility +
       salt/mineral crust (salt = food preservation and trade good)."

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/desert_world_design.md:34
      "| Salt flats / hardpan (open barren) | Perfect flat build/landing pad
       + visibility + salt/mineral crust | Water; food; cover - total
       exposure | Evaporite chems / rare salts | Zero cover ..."

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/resource_terrain_matrix.html:237
      "Salt-flat scrape-crust: salt = food-preservation and trade; rare
       evaporite chemicals = ③."

  And the guardrail that shapes the def, quoted so nobody relaxes it later:

    design/Jawa/mods/required_mods.md:459
      "Terrain guardrail: reject any farmable-anywhere / high-fertility /
       passive-resource-generating terrain (anti-exponential violation
       dressed as scenery). Cosmetic sand/rock/salt = fine."

    design/Jawa/worldbuilding/setup_checklist.md:142-144
      "Desert terrain flavor: terrain QUANTITY already solved by the stack;
       gap is desert DENSITY (sand/salt/hardpan variants). ... Guardrail:
       reject farmable-anywhere / passive-resource terrain."

  So this terrain is COSMETIC ONLY. fertility 0, no yield, no comp, nothing
  the player can farm or harvest. The salt as a TRADE GOOD is deliberately
  NOT implemented here; that is the terrain treasure work (desert_world_design
  §3B, "an evaporite harvest field"), which is v2.

  ===========================================================================
  ART: REUSED, AND ART IS OWED
  ===========================================================================
  texturePath is Odyssey's own "Terrain/Surfaces/DryLakeBed", a pale cracked
  hardpan. Verified to exist twice over:

    Odyssey/Defs/TerrainDefs/Terrain_Natural.xml:263
        <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/DryLakeBed</texturePath>
    Odyssey/AssetBundles/resources_odyssey.manifest:682
        Assets/Data/Odyssey/Textures/Terrain/Surfaces/DryLakeBed.psd

  Core and the DLCs ship NO loose Textures folder at all, so a bundle
  manifest string is the strongest offline proof available for a shipped
  terrain texture. Note also that NOTHING in Core, any DLC or any biome def
  references DryLakeBed (grep of the whole Data tree: three hits, all of them
  its own declaration), so this art is currently unused by the game.

  A purpose drawn salt texture is OWED and is v2. Reuse is the v1 route
  precisely because the v1 gate is "a human sees it", and a texture path that
  does not resolve is an invisible failure.

  <color> pushes the cracked hardpan toward evaporite white. If a future
  1.x quietly stops honouring <color> on natural terrain, the DryLakeBed
  texture is STILL visually distinct from Sand and SoftSand, so this override
  degrades to "slightly less white" rather than to "invisible". That was the
  deciding reason for reusing DryLakeBed instead of tinting Sand.

  ===========================================================================
  WHY ParentName IS NaturalTerrainBase AND NOT Sand
  ===========================================================================
  Odyssey's DryLakeBed uses ParentName="Sand", which is legal because Sand
  carries Name="Sand" (Core Terrain_Natural.xml:280). We do not copy that.
  Inheriting from Sand would also inherit its <tools> (KickMaterialInEyes ->
  SandInEyes) and <generatedFilth>Filth_Sand</generatedFilth>, neither of
  which belongs on a hard mineral crust. NaturalTerrainBase is genuinely
  Abstract="True" (Core Terrain_Natural.xml:4) and supplies Walkable,
  natural=true and the pollution overlay fields; everything else below is
  written out explicitly and each field is copied from a def read on disk.

  Field by field provenance, Core/Defs/TerrainDefs/Terrain_Natural.xml:
    edgeType FadeRough        Sand :286
    categoryType Sand         Sand :287
    renderPrecedence          Sand 350 :288, SoftSand 352; 351 sits between
    pathCost                  Soil is 2, Sand 4; 0 = a hard flat pan
    statBases / scatterType   Sand :290-296
    affordances               Sand :297-303 (Walkable comes from the parent)
    fertility 0               SoftSand :337, DryLakeBed Odyssey :269
    takeFootprints            Sand :304
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>Jawa_SaltCrust</defName>
    <label>salt crust</label>
    <description>A hard evaporite pan left by water that dried out an age ago. The crust is flat, blinding at midday, and takes a footprint like chalk. It will grow nothing whatsoever, but it is the firmest ground on the planet and you can see anything coming across it long before it arrives.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/DryLakeBed</texturePath>
    <color>(232, 230, 218)</color>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <categoryType>Sand</categoryType>
    <renderPrecedence>351</renderPrecedence>
    <pathCost>0</pathCost>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>-2</Beauty>
      <BeautyOutdoors>0</BeautyOutdoors>
      <Cleanliness>-1</Cleanliness>
    </statBases>
    <scatterType>SoftDry</scatterType>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Heavy</li>
      <li>Diggable</li>
    </affordances>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <takeFootprints>true</takeFootprints>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
